# Python Port Plan — Step 10_standard_tool_library

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/10_standard_tool_library` — three new built-in
tool modules (`Tools::FileSystem`, `Tools::Shell`, `Tools::Mud`) plus new
`working_dir:`/`allowed_commands:`/`shell_timeout:`/`mud:` keyword
arguments on `Boukensha.run`/`.repl` — to Python at
`week1_baseline/python/10_standard_tool_library`. Same conventions as
prior steps — see [`00_config`](00_config) for the full rationale; this
doc covers what's new. Note: `09_global_executable` was **not** ported
(pure RubyGems packaging, no logic to mirror — see
[`09_global_executable`](09_global_executable)), so this step's "previous
step" for orientation/carry-forward purposes is `08_the_repl_loop`, the
last step that *was* ported.

## Decisions carried forward (unchanged)

| Question | Decision |
|---|---|
| Port fidelity | Literal mirror of the Ruby architecture |
| Ruby vs Python | Run alongside each other; Ruby stays untouched *except* real bug fixes explicitly authorized |
| Tooling | `uv` + `pyproject.toml` |
| Python package name | `boukensha` |
| Copy-forward | Byte-identical-Ruby-source files copied from the already-fixed Python file of whichever prior step's Ruby source actually matches |

## Decided with the user (2026-07-24)

**`Tools::Mud` gets a full port**, not deferred or stubbed. Ruby's
`Tools::Mud` depends on this project's own `mud_manager` gem
(`week0_explore/mud_manager`) for the telnet session — there is no Python
equivalent anywhere in the repo (`play-mud`'s `scripts/mud.py` is a
standalone daemon script, not an importable session class). Chose the
"full port now" option over deferring or stubbing, so Python reaches full
parity with Ruby's live MUD-playing capability this step.

**Placement decision (mine, not asked — a packaging detail, not a behavior
question):** rather than standing up a brand-new top-level Python package
mirroring `week0_explore/mud_manager` (its own `pyproject.toml`, `uv sync`,
etc. — meaningfully bigger scope, and only used by this one step's
`Tools::Mud`), the ported session/primitives code lives directly inside
this step's own package as `boukensha/mud_session.py` and
`boukensha/mud_primitives.py`. This is a packaging-topology difference,
not a behavior difference — the Ruby gem's `Session`/`Primitives` classes
are still mirrored faithfully, just vendored rather than split into a
separate installable dependency. Also: `Primitives` in Ruby defines ~50
command-builder methods; only the ~22 `Tools::Mud` actually calls are
ported (`look`, `examine`, `info_self`, `move`, `flee`, `set_position`,
`track`, `attack`, `skill_strike`, `consider`, `say_local`,
`say_targeted`, `say_channel`, `get`, `drop`, `put`, `equip`, `consume`,
`cast`, `use_magic_item`, `shop`, `practice`, `save_char`) — matching
"port what's used," not the full unused surface.

## New for this step: real Ruby bugs already fixed (not just the standard regression set)

Both found and fixed during live review/testing of the Ruby source *before*
this plan was written — see `docs/journal/1_week1.md`'s `10_standard_tool_library`
entry for the full incident writeups:

1. **`Tools::Shell`'s `allowed_commands` allow-list was bypassable.** It
   only checked a command's first whitespace token, but `Open3.capture2e`
   with a single string argument runs the whole command through a real
   shell — `"git status; rm -rf ~"` passed an allow-list containing only
   `"git"`. Fixed in Ruby by rejecting shell metacharacters
   (`; & | > < \` $` or a newline) outright whenever an allow-list is set,
   verified live (injection blocked, legitimate single commands still
   pass). **Ported with the identical fix** — see `tools/shell.py` below.
2. **`boukensha.gemspec`'s `mud_manager` dependency doesn't resolve** — it
   has never been published to RubyGems, only exists locally. Fixed in
   Ruby with a `Gemfile` `path:` override. **No Python equivalent needed**
   — the placement decision above (vendoring `mud_session.py`/
   `mud_primitives.py` directly in this step's package) sidesteps the
   issue entirely; there's no external dependency to resolve.

